{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_04f853ae3f8b6eaa0169bb447afd688191ad48d6c4908e9adb", "created": 1773880443, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"MyChart and Patient Portals for Test Results & Care-Team Messaging\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on digital patient-access tools—particularly a general PATIENT PORTAL concept and ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS—with MyChart featured as a concrete example used to manage a health record account, track appointments, view visit documentation, and exchange portal messages with clinical staff. The relationships emphasize two primary functions: (1) enabling patients to access and review clinical information (test results and visit notes) through portals, and (2) supporting communication via portal messaging while explicitly warning that nurses and physicians may not see messages immediately, making portal messaging inappropriate for urgent or time-sensitive concerns. Overall, the community depicts a patient-facing health information and communication workflow whose main risk is delayed clinical response and misinterpretation of complex results when viewed before clinician discussion.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Patient portals serve as the core patient-facing mechanism for receiving and reviewing clinical information\",\n      \"explanation\": \"PATIENT PORTAL is described as a digital system that gives patients direct access to their health information, including test results (such as imaging reports) and clinical documentation. The community positions the portal as a key pathway by which results may be delivered to patients, sometimes even before discussion with the care team. [Data: Entities (2465); Relationships (7347, 7348)]\\n\\nThis information-access function is reinforced by related activities: signing up for a portal account and reviewing results/notes via the portal. These activities show the portal’s role not only as a repository, but as an operational tool that supports the patient’s ongoing engagement with care information. [Data: Entities (3611, 3612); Relationships (7350, 7351)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Online patient portals are framed as a primary method for accessing test results, with MyChart as a representative implementation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS are explicitly characterized as a convenient way for patients to access test results and medical records, including the ability to view, download, or print information. This positions online portals as a central enabler of the broader activity of ACCESSING TEST RESULTS. [Data: Entities (422, 2392); Relationships (4217)]\\n\\nWithin this ecosystem, MYCHART (HEALTH RECORD ACCOUNT) is presented as an example/type of portal account used to track appointments and communicate with the care team. This connects the general concept of online portals to a specific portal product/workflow used for day-to-day care management. [Data: Entities (1341, 422); Relationships (2196)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Visit notes are a distinct information asset made available through portals to help patients track care\",\n      \"explanation\": \"VISIT NOTES are described as documentation from appointments/visits that may be available through a patient portal, indicating that portals provide more than discrete results and can include narrative clinical documentation that supports continuity and patient understanding over time. [Data: Entities (3600); Relationships (7348)]\\n\\nThe workflow also includes the explicit action of REVIEWING RESULTS AND NOTES, tying the presence of visit notes in portals to patient behavior and ongoing care tracking. This suggests a routine review process that is facilitated by the portal’s availability of documentation. [Data: Entities (3612); Relationships (7351)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Portal messaging is an important communication channel, but delayed review by clinicians is a key operational constraint\",\n      \"explanation\": \"PORTAL MESSAGES are defined as communications sent through an online patient portal system such as MyChart, with the explicit caveat that these messages may not be immediately seen or reviewed by clinical staff. The community treats delayed visibility as a core characteristic of the messaging channel. [Data: Entities (1207, 1196); Relationships (12875)]\\n\\nThis constraint is repeated in clinician-specific relationships: messages may not be immediately seen by a NURSE or a PHYSICIAN, implying that portal messaging is asynchronous and subject to triage or queue-based workflows rather than real-time response. [Data: Entities (1194, 1195, 1207); Relationships (1924, 1925, 12876)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Safety and escalation guidance: portal messaging should not be used for urgent or time-sensitive needs\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community contains explicit guidance that, because portal messages may not be promptly reviewed, patients should not rely on portal messaging for urgent concerns and should instead use more immediate communication pathways (e.g., calling). This guidance is grounded both in the description of MyChart account messaging and in the nurse/physician message-review relationships. [Data: Entities (1341, 1194, 1195); Relationships (2199, 1924, 1925)]\\n\\nOperationally, this indicates a risk surface centered on expectation management: if patients assume rapid response where none is guaranteed, delays could affect time-sensitive care decisions. The data emphasizes clarifying communication pathways and appropriate channels for urgency. [Data: Entities (1194, 1341); Relationships (2199, 1924)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"A recurring risk theme is patient interpretation of complex results appearing before clinician discussion\",\n      \"explanation\": \"PATIENT PORTAL is described as a channel where test results—particularly imaging reports—may appear before the patient has discussed them with the care team, and patients are advised to review findings with their doctor to ensure appropriate interpretation and next steps. This highlights an interpretability risk when complex information is delivered directly to patients without contextual explanation. [Data: Entities (2465)]\\n\\nONLINE PATIENT PORTALS similarly note that results can be complex and may require clinician explanation, reinforcing that direct access increases transparency but can introduce misunderstanding without follow-up discussion. [Data: Entities (422)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Appointment management connects information access (results/notes) with care coordination via MyChart accounts\",\n      \"explanation\": \"APPOINTMENTS (MEDICAL) are described as scheduled visits for imaging, biopsies, and clinical evaluation, and the community links appointment tracking to MyChart (or a health record account). This establishes MyChart accounts as a coordination tool that complements information viewing and messaging. [Data: Entities (3630, 1341); Relationships (7435)]\\n\\nTaken together, the relationships show a unified portal-based workflow: patients sign up, access and review results and visit notes, track appointments, and message their care team—while needing to understand messaging latency and appropriate escalation routes. [Data: Entities (2465, 3611, 3612, 3630, 1196); Relationships (7350, 7351, 7347, 7348, 7435, +more)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 4.2,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact is moderate because these tools directly influence patient access to sensitive clinical information and care coordination, with the main risk being delayed responses and misinterpretation rather than malicious capability.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null, "reasoning_content": "**Explaining patient care communication impacts*